# MediKiosk: AI-Powered Digital Clinical Intake & Document Intelligence Platform
## Problem Statement & Technical Solution Document (Smart India Hackathon / Healthcare Innovation)

---

### 1. Executive Summary & Problem Analysis

#### 1.1 The Clinical History-Taking Bottleneck in Indian Hospitals
History taking — the structured elicitation of presenting complaints, history of present illness (HPI), past medical and surgical history, drug and allergy history, family and personal history, and review of systems — is the single most important diagnostic activity in clinical medicine. Classical evidence demonstrates that a rigorous clinical history yields the correct provisional diagnosis in 70–80% of cases, prior to physical examination or investigation.

In India's overburdened public healthcare institutions (AIIMS, State Medical Colleges, and District Hospitals), outpatient departments (OPDs) routinely register 4,000 to 10,000 patients daily. Consultation times average between **2 and 5 minutes per patient** (BMJ Open multi-country survey). Within this microscopic window, the physician must simultaneously elicit history, conduct physical examination, review messy paper files, formulate diagnoses, counsel the patient, and prescribe treatments.

#### 1.2 The AYUSH OPD Complexity
Ayurvedic OPDs face an even greater time deficit. Ayurvedic history taking is fundamentally holistic and personalized, demanding:
- **Trividha & Ashtavidha Pariksha** (Nadi, Mutra, Mala, Jihva, Shabda, Sparsha, Druk, Akriti)
- **Dashavidha Pariksha** (Prakriti, Vikriti, Sara, Samhanana, Pramana, Satmya, Sattva, Ahara Shakti, Vyayama Shakti, Vaya)
- **Agni & Koshtha Pariksha** (Mandagni, Tikshnagni, Vishamagni, Samagni; Krura, Mridu, Madhyama)
- **Ahara-Vihara Assessment** (Nidana, dietetics, sleep habits, emotional stress, and daily regimen)

Manually capturing this depth under heavy OPD load is virtually impossible, forcing clinicians to truncate the very assessment that defines Ayurvedic precision medicine.

#### 1.3 Documentation & Records Fragmentation
Indian patients carry disjointed physical paper records — handwritten OPD slips, NABL diagnostic lab sheets, and discharge summaries across multiple healthcare providers. Valuable consultation minutes are lost decoding unstructured paper. Furthermore, while the **Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM)** has deployed ABHA IDs and FHIR standards, the critical "first-mile" intake bottleneck at hospital gates remains unaddressed.

